  "subtitle": "The first CRT glass cockpit in an airliner: two color EADIs and EHSIs, a keyshow EFIS control panel, and a 'rising runway' symbol that grows up the screen as you descend",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum curates interfaces that changed how a human is embedded in a machine. The Boeing 757/767 cockpit was the first commercial airliner flight deck to drop the electromechanical flight instruments and the flight engineer's panel entirely, replacing them with two color CRTs driven by symbol generators and controlled from a small keyshow EFIS control panel. The interaction is a genuinely novel embodied human-factors design: color encodes state as a semantic machine (armed captions blue, captured green, deviation fixtures white-to-amber-and-flash), data declutters itself automatically, and a rising-runway symbol literally grows up the screen over your final 200 feet. This fills the aviation/glass-cockpit gap in the collection, which is otherwise entirely consumer, lab, and gaming interfaces.",
  "search_terms": ["Collins EFIS", "glass cockpit", "boeing 757", "EADI EHSI", "electronic flight instrument", "primary flight display 1982", "CRT flight display"],
  "research": {
    "title": "Rockwell Collins EFIS — Boeing 757/767 Glass Cockpit",
    "year": "1982\u20131983",
    "by": "Rockwell Collins / Collins Avionics (Cedar Rapids, IA)",
    "tags": ["HCI", "Aviation", "Display", "CRT", "Safety-critical", "Physical"],
    "overview": "The Boeing 757, which first flew on 19 February 1982, was certified on 21 December 1982 and entered revenue service on 1 January 1983 with Eastern Air Lines, became the first commercial airliner with a fully electronic CRT color 'glass cockpit' that removed the flight engineer's position to leave a two-pilot deck. Its Electronic Flight Instrument System (EFIS), built by Rockwell Collins, replaced the electro-mechanical attitude indicator, horizontal situation indicator, and the flight engineer's entire instrument panel with a pair of Electronic Attitude Director Indicators (EADI) and a pair of Electronic Horizontal Situation Indicators (EHSI), driven by symbol generators and controlled from small EFIS control panels. The same Collins EFIS went into the concurrent Boeing 767 (sharing a common type rating with the 757) and the 757/767-style cockpit architecture.\n\nThe reading model is the interaction. Each EADI superimposes airspeed tape and fast/slow pointer, altitude, radio altitude with a decision-height alert, Mach readout, ILS localizer and glideslope deviation, flight-director command bars, a pitch-limit symbol, a rising-runway symbol, and autopilot/autothrottle mode annunciations — around a simulated attitude sphere. Data is decluttered automatically (the glideslope scale appears only on approach; pitching beyond 30-60 degrees strips non-critical symbology). Color is a semantic state machine: an armed glideslope caption is blue and turns green on capture; deviation scales turn white-to-amber and flash on ILS excursion; the decision-height readout enlarges and flashes amber at the alert. Physical control lives on the EFIS Control Panel, a compact rack box per pilot with keyshow pushbutton switches for EHSI modes (MAP / VOR / ILS / PLAN / CENTRE-MAP), a range selector, a weather-radar switch, and a decision-height set/reset.",
    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "Two CRTs replace six instruments and a flight engineer",
        "text": "The 757/767 cockpit consolidated attestitude, heading, flight-director, navigation, and systems data onto two square color CRTs per pilot plus a modest center panel — eliminating the flight engineer station. This is the single most consequential interface consolidation in civil-aviation history and the origin of the airliner 'glass cockpit'."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Color as a state machine",
        "text": "The human-factors novelty is that color has fixed meaning instead of decoration. An armed caption is blue; on capture it turns green. Deviation scales progress white-to-amber and flash on an excursion. The decision-height readout enlarges and flashes amber at the alert. The pilot is trained to read status off the color of a symbol rather than its position alone."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Declutter and the rising runway",
        "text": "The display automatically removes non-critical symbology by flight condition: the glideslope scale appears only on approach, and extreme pitch strips extraneous data. The most memorable cue is the 'rising runway' symbol — a graphic runway wedge that physically grows up the screen across the last 200 feet of radio altitude, ticking toward the airplane glyph. A descending aircraft feels its destination approach on screen."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The EFIS control panel",
        "text": "The physical control surface is a compact keyshow panel per pilot: pushbutton mode switches (MAP / VOR / ILS / PLAN / CENTRE-MAP), a range selector, a weather-radar switch, and a decision-height set/reset. The pilot steers what the CRTs show with a handful of physical keys before ever touching the automation."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where it sits in the museum",
        "text": "The museum is nearly all consumer, laboratory, and game interfaces. The glass cockpit is the museum's first aircraft HCI — a safety-critical, professionally embedded display race, the moment the picture tube replaced the frozen needle-and-ball gauge in the most demanding real-time interface a human operates."
      }
